6045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484149 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484149 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186483875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484149 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "88c428fa-eb9a-5abc-94ad-cb031dd35e32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484236 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484149 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484236 #edb/ref 1069 "q-70957" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484236 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484236 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the sphere $(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}+{z}^{2}=36$ and the line \n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=t+1 \\\\ y &=2t+2, \\\\ z &=t+6\\end{aligned}\\right.$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186484236 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7819f027-d28d-5660-b11c-cd0f95f720fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484236 #edb/ref 1074 "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the sphere, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =36 \\\\ {((t+1)-1)}^{2}+{((2t+2)-2)}^{2}+{(t+6)}^{2} & =36 \\\\ {t}^{2}+4{t}^{2}+({t}^{2}+12t+36) & =36 \\\\ 6{t}^{2}+12t+36 & =36 \\\\ 6{t}^{2}+12t & =0 \\\\ t(t+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=0$ and $t=-2.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=(0)+1=1 \\\\ y &=2(0)+2=2 \\\\ z &=(0)+6=6,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(1,2,6).$\n\n- For $t=-2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=(-2)+1=-1 \\\\ y &=2(-2)+2=-2 \\\\ z &=(-2)+6=4,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(-1,-2,4).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(1,2,6)$ and $(-1,-2,4).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186484244 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484244 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73219" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484244 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484244 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{12}=1$ and the line \n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-3t \\\\ y &=2t, \\\\ z &=-2t\\end{aligned}\\right.$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186484244 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "83fca566-7995-55f0-a5c4-c64563aea218" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484244 #edb/ref 1074 "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{12} & =1 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =12 \\\\ 4(-3t)^{2}+2(2t)^{2}+(-2t)^{2} & =12 \\\\ 36{t}^{2}+8{t}^{2}+4{t}^{2} & =12 \\\\ 48{t}^{2} & =12 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $t=\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-\\frac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-3(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y &=2(-\\frac{1}{2})=-1 \\\\ z &=-2(-\\frac{1}{2})=1,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(\\frac{3}{2},-1,1).$\n\n- For $t=\\frac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-3(\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y &=2(\\frac{1}{2})=1 \\\\ z &=-2(\\frac{1}{2})=-1,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(-\\frac{3}{2},1,-1).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(\\frac{3}{2},-1,1)$ and $(-\\frac{3}{2},1,-1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186484270 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484104 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484270 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71129" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484270 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484270 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1$ and the line \n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2t \\\\ y &=2-t, \\\\ z &=t-1\\end{aligned}\\right.$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186484270 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bf7120f6-4fae-5d19-b842-ba88886669a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484270 #edb/ref 1074 "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2} & =8 \\\\ (2t)^{2}+4(2-t)^{2}+2(t-1)^{2} & =8 \\\\ 4{t}^{2}+4(4-4t+{t}^{2})+2({t}^{2}-2t+1) & =8 \\\\ 4{t}^{2}+16-16t+4{t}^{2}+2{t}^{2}-4t+2 & =8 \\\\ 10{t}^{2}-20t+18 & =8 \\\\ 10{t}^{2}-20t+10 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}-2t+1 & =0 \\\\ (t-1)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solution $t=1.$\n\nNow, we substitute our solution back into the equations of the line.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2(1)=2 \\\\ y &=2-1=1 \\\\ z &=1-1=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(2,1,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186495599 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{2}=1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495599 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495599 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2903bd31-59f2-5edf-ba78-0b700636216f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495763 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{4}=1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495763 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495763 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "72fcd5f6-74c1-548c-b798-b9f86c8b5a52" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495990 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{2}=1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495990 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495990 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d9e331d6-2b84-5961-a705-7c0440233497" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496016 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{{